<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Barclay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1648">1648</a>–<a href="/years/1690">1690</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Barclay was a Scottish Quaker theologian whose Apology for the True Christian Divinity became the classic statement of Quaker belief. In 1682 he was one of the 24 proprietors who bought East Jersey, and he was made its governor for life, though he never went to America and ruled through deputies. He encouraged Scottish settlement in the colony.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Barclay was born at Gordonstoun in Moray on 23 December 1648, the son of David Barclay, first laird of Ury, who had served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a>, and of Katherine Gordon, daughter of Sir Robert Gordon, 1st Baronet of Gordonstoun. He finished his education at the Scots College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where his uncle was Rector; the uncle offered to make him his heir if he stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and joined the Roman Catholic Church. Instead, after returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, he followed his father into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Religious Society of Friends</a> in 1667.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Barclay began writing in defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> with Truth cleared of Calumnies (1670), a reply to William Mitchell, and A Catechism and Confession of Faith (1673). His central view was that all people can be illuminated by the Inward Light of Christ. His fifteen Theses Theologiae, which set that light above reason and scripture, led to a public debate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> in which each side claimed victory. The Apology appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1676 and in his own English translation in 1678. He supported George Fox in the controversies of the 1670s.</p><p>In 1670 he married the Quaker Christian Mollison, daughter of Gilbert Mollison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>; they had three sons and four daughters. He travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, once with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> and George Fox, met Elisabeth, Princess Palatine, and was imprisoned several times, though each time he soon regained his liberty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Barclay had much influence with James II, and he is said to have visited the king to seek terms with William of Orange. As absentee governor of East Jersey he governed through deputies: Thomas Rudyard from 16 September 1682, Gawen Lawrie from 27 July 1683, Lord Neill Campbell in 1686 and Andrew Hamilton from 10 December 1686. Under Rudyard the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, Essex, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a> and Monmouth were set up, and in 1686 the assembly rejected the Fundamental Constitutions drafted by the proprietors. Barclay died at Ury on 3 October 1690. His grandson David Barclay of Youngsbury was one of the founders of what became Barclays Bank.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Barclay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To differing extents Friends avoid creeds and hierarchical structures, and their doctrines have only at times been set down in statements of faith, such as <b>Robert Barclay</b>'s An Apology for the True Christian Divinity (1678) and the Richmond Declaration of Faith (1887).</blockquote><a href="/movement/quakers">Quakers · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>After Carteret's death his heirs sold the colony in 1682 to 24 proprietors, many of them Quakers and Scots, including William Penn and <b>Robert Barclay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-jersey">East Jersey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gordonstoun, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Ury House, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.7,-3.37&amp;year=1675" aria-label="Gordonstoun on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4478 212 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Barclay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4928 482h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4928 482h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.7,-3.37&amp;year=1675">What was here in 1675? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
